IMPORTANT NOTICE: THIRD-PARTY SOURCE CODE & HISTORICAL ARTIFACTS
===============================================================================
The historical operating system source code contained in the individual OS
subdirectories is NOT covered by the CC BY 4.0 license above. Each subdirectory
retains its original upstream copyright, license, or proprietary status.

See THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md for complete, authoritative documentation.

Critical restrictions and legal boundaries:

1. Nokia Bell Labs Research Unix (V8/, V9/, V10/):
   Covered by Alcatel-Lucent USA Inc. / Nokia Bell Laboratories 2017 Covenant
   Not to Assert Copyright for NON-COMMERCIAL research and educational use only.
   Commercial copying, distribution, or derived works are strictly prohibited.

2. Linux 0.01 (Linux-0.01/):
   Subject to Linus Torvalds's original September 1991 non-commercial notice
   (RELNOTES-0.01) which strictly forbids charging fees or commercial
   distribution. This release was never retroactively relicensed to the GPL.

3. Commercial Proprietary Systems (SunOS-4.1.4/, Ultrix-3.1/):
   Proprietary commercial operating systems owned respectively by Oracle America,
   Inc. (Sun Microsystems) and Hewlett Packard Enterprise (Digital Equipment
   Corporation). Neither vendor has granted a public redistribution license.
   Retained strictly for historical scholarship, research, and emulation.

4. Composite Developer Releases (NFSv2/):
   Sun Microsystems proprietary developer release (December 1985) overlaid on
   modified 4.2BSD source code, requiring an original Sun developer license.

5. Ancient UNIX Scope (Caldera 2002 Grant):
   The 2002 Caldera open-source release strictly applies to AT&T/Bell Labs code
   in 16-bit PDP-11 V1 through V7 and 32-bit 32V. Independent third-party
   contributions in derivative trees (AUSAM, Interdata 7/32, V7M, BBN, SRI)
   are not licensed by Caldera and retain their respective author terms.
